      "locator": {
        "page": 2,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p2_s3"
      },
      "text": "Binary path MaintenaceSrv32.exe or MaintenaceSrv64.exe The dropper chooses a random name when installing the communication and wiper modules to the system.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_25",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "PaloAlto_Shamoon3-Targets-Oil-and-Gas(12-13-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 3,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p3_s3"
      },
      "text": "The wiper could be configured to use a file to overwrite the files on the disk using the ‘F’ configuration flag, as we saw images used to overwrite files in previous Shamoon attacks.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_32",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "PaloAlto_Shamoon3-Targets-Oil-and-Gas(12-13-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 4,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p4_s1"
      },
      "text": "The communications module is responsible for reaching out to hardcoded URLs to communicate with the C2 server, but like previous Disttrack samples, this communication module does not contain functional C2 domains to use in the URLs.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_33",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "PaloAlto_Shamoon3-Targets-Oil-and-Gas(12-13-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 4,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p4_s2"
      },
      "text": "If it did, it would create a URL with a parameter named ‘selection’ followed by system information and the contents of the ‘averbh_noav.pnf’ file, as seen here:",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_35",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "PaloAlto_Shamoon3-Targets-Oil-and-Gas(12-13-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 4,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p4_s4"
      },
      "text": "Command Description T Opens the ‘\\inf\\mdmnis5tQ1.pnf’ file and writes a supplied date to the file.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_36",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "PaloAlto_Shamoon3-Targets-Oil-and-Gas(12-13-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 4,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p4_s5"
      },
      "text": "The ‘\\inf\\mdmnis5tQ1.pnf’ file is used by another associated module to this communications module that is responsible for wiping the system.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_39",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "PaloAlto_Shamoon3-Targets-Oil-and-Gas(12-13-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 4,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p4_s8"
      },
      "text": "However, this older date is still effective as the Disttrack dropper will install and run the wiper module as long as the system date is after the wipe date.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_43",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "PaloAlto_Shamoon3-Targets-Oil-and-Gas(12-13-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "rule",
        "sentence_id": "p1_r1"
      },
      "text": "On December 10, a new variant of the Disttrack malware was submitted to VirusTotal (SHA256:c3ab58b3154e5f5101ba74fccfd27a9ab445e41262cdf47e8cc3be7416a5904f) that shares a considerable amount of code with the Disttrack malware used in the Shamoon 2 attacks in 2016 and 2017 that we previously published here, here, and here. While we could not identify the impacted organization from the malware, today Saipem disclosed they were attacked. In previous attacks, we were able to determine the impacted organization based on the domain names and credentials used by the Disttrack tool to spread to other systems on the network. However, that functionality was missing from this sample. Unlike past Shamoon attacks, this particular Disttrack wiper would not overwrite files with an image. Instead it would overwrite the MBR, partitions, and files on the system with randomly generated data.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "note": "rule or indicator snippet evidence",
      "source": "document"
